  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs) are equally effective as first-line medications for lowering blood pressure, but ARBs are generally better tolerated with fewer side effects.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs are generally better tolerated with fewer side effects"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy and Guidelines"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Equal Blood Pressure Control: Both drug classes inhibit the renin-angiotensin system and provide comparable reductions in blood pressure and primary cardiovascular outcomes (such as heart attack and stroke)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Guideline Status: Major guidelines, such as those from the American College of Cardiology / American Heart Association and European societies, list both ACE inhibitors and ARBs as top-tier, first-line choices for initial hypertension treatment.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "American College of Cardiology / American Heart Association",
                "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Tolerability"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Persistent Dry Cough: ACE inhibitors block the breakdown of bradykinin, which causes a nagging, dry cough in up to 20% of patients. ARBs do not affect bradykinin, making this cough rare."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: ACE inhibitors carry a small but serious risk of dangerous tissue swelling (angioedema) in the face, lips, or throat. This risk is significantly lower with ARBs."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Adherence: Because of fewer side effects like the dry cough, patients are less likely to stop taking ARBs compared to ACE inhibitors."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Shared Precautions"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Hyperkalemia: Both classes can raise blood levels of potassium, requiring periodic monitoring of kidney function and electrolytes."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pregnancy Warning: Neither ACE inhibitors nor ARBs should be used during pregnancy due to the risk of severe fetal harm."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do Not Combine: Taking an ACE inhibitor and an ARB at the same time is strongly discouraged because it increases the risk of high potassium and kidney injury without adding extra benefit."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Other health conditions you have (like kidney disease or diabetes)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Any current medications you take"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help explain which option fits your medical history best."
      }
    ],
